[Music]  El Salvador is one of the deadliest  places on earth  the tiny Central American country is in  the grip of a war among rival gangs and  the police successive governments have  attempted to control the gangs using  force cracking down in the streets and  filling the prison's  last year more than 10 people were  murdered every day  gangsters tattoo their allegiance on  their skin membership is for life in El  Salvador they say the only way to leave  the gang is in a body bag those who  attempt to leave face social stigma and  the threat of execution and other gang  members or the police rehabilitation  programs are practically non-existent  but in the capital city San Salvador a  religious group is trying to help the  gangsters swap the gang for God if God  could change someone like me someone  that has done so many wrong things why  not change all these other men  [Music]  when Wilfredo Gomez was growing up El  Salvador was in the midst of a bloody  civil war that left one in sixty  Salvadorians dead and one in four  displaced  [Applause]  in 1987 gomez like hundreds of thousands  of others fled to the United States on  the streets of Los Angeles he found a  home with the gangs once I get into the  hood I decided to join the gang and I  had to find a way to survive to make  money me so Isis sell heroin Gomez  joined 18th Street named after the area  in LA where most of the gang members  lived they fought for territory with a  rival Salvadorian gang ms-13 a lot of my  friends that I grew up with and they're  joined the gang almost at the same time  then were killed during that war it  wasn't long before he had a run-in with  the police and was put behind bars I got  busted for selling narcotics to survive  and using that at the same time in a bid  to reduce gang violence the Clinton  administration in 1996 passed the law  that meant immigrants convicted of  crimes with a prison sentence of more  than a year would be deported I remember  my mom mentioning a you need to be  careful they'll be pouring people now be  smart stay away from trouble  thousands of gang members were sent back  to El Salvador  including many members of ms-13 and 18th  Street who took their brutal battle for  the streets with them the number of  deported soon rivaled the size of El  Salvador sixteen thousand stroke police  after almost 20 years in the u.s. half  of which was in prison gomez saw the  deportation as his ticket to freedom the  one thing I wanted the most was to be  free so I signed three days later I was  on the plane my way back warrior just  three months after he arrived he was  back in prison serving ten years for  stealing a security guards machine gun  I remember going into prison and they've  just had a massacre the smell of blood  was still in there I'm talk about 150  persons in one cell had sleep there  shower there what are the bathroom there  from his crowded prison cell Gomez found  an unlikely salvation the Christian  people they asked to pray for me so I  told the guide ok pray for me and they  need something happened it's like wine  like a little kid the prayers had a  profound effect on Gomez I had spent  nearly 16 years of my life in prison and  all along I knew that I needed help but  I didn't know who to run to  I had no family the guy that was praying  for me as me following the received  Jesus and I told him that after joining  the church Gomez began to convert other  gangsters to evangelical Christianity  [Applause]  no kidding this oportunidad in 2016  he helped persuade the prison director  to establish a separate area where  religious inmates could live and pray in  peace away from other gang members this  was an attractive proposition and  hundreds of prisoners followed Gomez  some were genuine converts others just  wanted better prison conditions  [Applause]  I have never seen so many guys being  impacted by the gospel guys all tatted  from their face singing praises to the  Lord when I first joined the church it  was about a hundred men in there and  little by little that 100 years make  right now it's over in my hand it's  something was happening I mean gang gang  members don't change just like that in  early 2017 Gomez was given parole in San  Salvador he feared that if he left  prison he would fall back into the gang  on the outside there are a few prospects  for anyone linked to the gang to earn  money legitimately am I surprised there  was someone waiting at the door a  Christian and they had heard about me  through a guy and they had control  before me and he had told them that I  was one of the leaders in prison with  that Christian movement so they brought  me here to this church they made food  for me  celebrating my freedom that was big for  me  since leaving prison Gomez has helped  other newly released gang members find  refuge in a tiny Church in San Salvador  the inmates who convert inside prison  struggle to stay away from the gang once  released only seven former gangsters of  church members on the outside the rest  find their way back to the gang those  who do join the church have a tough  existence they have no money no  prospects in no place in society being  annexed gang member here in Ostrava door  and facing society's rejection it's the  biggest obstacle that I faced so far no  dream of Purdue's Familia mophir deal  the totem of perdido aim of didn't even  wait at all pero Mira a duro pero ahora  que movie near Caminos do not have n  decir una manera  [Music]  yes I made wrong choices I mean my  mistakes but you have me yours  maybe my mistakes is that I put ink on  me maybe your mistake was that you  cheated on your husband maybe my mistake  was then once upon a time I took a life  either way there are mistakes maybe  mines are bigger than yours but I'm  still a human these men once devoted to  gangs now claim to be devoted to the  church in many ways the church is  similar to the gang  both have strict rules a hierarchy and  demand obedience solving El Salvador's  gang problem will take more than  converting a handful of gangsters to  Christianity the question facing El  Salvador in the long term isn't whether  an individual gang member can change but  where the society will create the  conditions that mean they can leave the  gang for good  [Music]  you

